<p>As someone who both maintains their own Linux server <em>and</em> dabbles in web development<sup id="fnref-41982-paternity"><a href="https://sixcolors.com/link/2026/10/the-one-guy-who-manages-most-of-php-distribution-is-a-volunteer-of-course/#fn-41982-paternity" class="jetpack-footnote" title="Read footnote.">1</a></sup>, I&#8217;d always noticed the update repository for PHP was surprisingly just called <code>ppa:ondrej/php</code> and wondered why exactly that was.</p>
<p>Turns out it&#8217;s named for Ondřej Surý the one (largely) unpaid volunteer who maintains it. As if he wasn&#8217;t <a href="https://xkcd.com/2347/">load-bearing enough</a>, he became  even more significant the other day when <a href="https://www.nsa.gov/press-room/press-releases-statements/press-release-view/article/4630238/nsa-advisory-critical-patches-recommended-for-php-vulnerabilities/">a massive PHP vulnerability</a> was uncovered.</p>
<p><em>The Atlantic</em>&#8216;s Charlie Warzel <a href="https://www.theatlantic.com/technology/2026/10/man-who-saved-internet-php-vulnerability/689123/">interviewed Surý about the situation</a>, and he points out some hard truths:</p>
<blockquote><p>
  Surý: When I started doing open-source, it was fun. And it kind of still is. But the environment has shifted. Open-source sometimes feels like exploitation. What I would like to see is to find a new model where people working on open-source would be awarded for their work and the hours and hours they put into it from the people who most benefit from their work. I would like companies to realize that this is exploitation. Any exploitation is not sustainable. And it will end up badly if this continues, because the people doing the open-source will stop being motivated to actually do anything in the end.
</p></blockquote>
<p>A reminder, as that XKCD comic linked above so saliently illustrates, that the massive profit-seeking ventures of the Internet are all too often built on the backs of unpaid labor.</p>
